  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 234 AM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nUpper level ridging across the Rockies and Desert Southwest on \nThursday will flatten out across the Northern and Central Plains \nFriday and into the weekend. Surface high pressure over the Great \nLakes and middle Mississippi Valley on Thursday will bring highs \nnear normal from 80 to 85 degrees. Some convection is expected to \noccur east of the upper ridge axis into western Nebraska, with at \nleast a slight chance for showers and storms during the evening and \novernight. Friday through Sunday, the upper ridge will flatten out \nas an upper trough moves across the southern Canadian Prairies. This \nwill bring a quasi-zonal flow aloft and above normal temperatures \nfrom 85 to the low 90s Friday, near 90 to the mid 90s Saturday, and \nupper 80s to lower 90s Sunday. Southerly winds 15 to 35 mph are \nexpected Friday, with lighter southwesterly winds Saturday and \nSunday. The more active weather will reside to our north across the \nDakota. However, a slight chance to low chance POPS will extend \nsouth into western Nebraska, mainly during the evening and overnight \nhours each day. By Monday, and easterly low level flow and highs \nfalling back into the mid to upper 80s. \n\nDespite daily opportunities for showers and thunderstorms, NBM 48 \nhour probabilities for at least 0.25\" ending Saturday morning is \nonly 15 to 25 percent and only 5 to 15 percent for the 48 hour \nperiod ending Monday morning.",
